+## Evidence Provenance: Local Review Evidence vs Trusted Remote CI
+
+| Evidence type | Source | Trust model | How to interpret in review |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| local evidence is review evidence | Generated during Palari review workflow: reviewer notes, specialist reports, scope-check output, ticket reports, packet content. The agent or reviewer who performed the work produces it, not an independent system. | Trusted by process — relies on human reviewer diligence, role scoping, and the accepted-ticket workflow that limits who can produce artifacts. | Examine alongside trusted remote CI to confirm that reviewer claims are not contradicted by automated checks. Local evidence alone does not independently prove safety, speed, performance, or model quality — it documents what the reviewer observed and decided. |
+| Trusted remote CI | Generated by externally-hardened CI infrastructure: GitHub Actions workflow runs, attestations, signed provenance, and integrity manifests that are harder for a single agent or reviewer to forge. | Trusted by infrastructure — the execution environment, identity binding, and log integrity are managed by a separate system with its own audit trail. | Use as the baseline integrity check before relying on local review evidence. If a trusted remote CI gate fails or is missing, that signal should block acceptance regardless of what local review evidence claims. Both passing together is stronger than either alone. |
